# The project video, as a cue sheet.
#
# Every frame is the real games being played by the real autopilot, rendered
# through the real cell shader -- this is the plugin running, not a mock-up.
# Render with:
#
#   ./build/coinopgl --sequence /tmp/co-frames --script tools/video.cues \
#                    --size 1920x1080 --seconds 57 --fps 30
#
# Syntax: `T Name=V` sets at a time, `T1..T2 Name=V1..V2` ramps between two
# (eased, because a parameter that starts and stops abruptly reads as a cut).
#
# Values are the 0..1 the host sees, except Game, Palette, Fit and Aspect, which
# are indices -- the same split the plugin itself has, see Controls.h.
#
# ## v0.3.0: Flapper, and the eight this cut inherited
#
# The v0.1.0 cut ran through the original five and is still up. The v0.2.0 cut
# showed the eight that release added -- and is being replaced by this one rather
# than left alongside it, because it is hours old and two near-identical videos
# hours apart is the case the seven-day rule exists to prevent.
#
# Replacing it means this cut has to carry what that one carried. So it is the
# eight, unchanged and in their original order, with Flapper in front of them.
# Flapper leads because it is the new game and because it is the fastest thing
# here to read: vertical bars with holes in them, and one cell going through.
# Everything after it is shifted by five and a half seconds and otherwise
# untouched -- there was nothing wrong with that order and re-cutting it would
# only risk it.
#
# Changing `Game` restarts the simulation (Sim::SetGame), so every segment is a
# game from its first tick. That is why Speed is pushed up in the segments whose
# interest accumulates -- Stacker has to build a stack and Trails has to lay
# trail, and neither has forty seconds to do it in.
#
# The pixel-map ending stays, because it is still the thing the plugin is *for*.

# ------------------------------------------------- 0s: Flapper, Phosphor
# One button against gravity, through the gaps in a wall that scrolls past. It
# opens the cut because it is the game this release added and because it is the
# one that needs no explaining -- the shape of the obstacle tells you the rule.
#
# Difficulty low and Skill high on purpose. The ramp makes this game end, and it
# ends faster the harder it starts; a five-second shot wants the flier threading
# holes for all five seconds rather than dying at three and restarting into a
# fresh field.
#
# The field is full from the first frame, which it was not when this was written
# -- see FillField in Flapper.cpp. Before that fix the first two seconds of this
# very shot were a black rectangle with one white cell in it.
0.0   Game=13
0.0   Palette=0
0.0   Speed=0.55
0.0   Skill=0.95
0.0   Difficulty=0.35
0.0   Grid=0.415
0.0   Seed=0.25
0.0   Round=0.25
0.0   Gap=0.16
0.0   Glow=0.50
0.0   React=0.40

# ------------------------------------------------------------ 5.5s: Chase, Amber
# A maze carved fresh at every reset, four pursuers, and the loops that make
# being chased survivable. Amber because the pellets have to read as the thing
# you want and the pursuers as the thing you do not.
5.5   Game=6
5.5   Palette=1
5.5   Speed=0.62
5.5   Skill=0.88
5.5   Difficulty=0.45
5.5   Grid=0.415
5.5   Seed=0.25
5.5   Round=0.25
5.5   Gap=0.18
5.5   Glow=0.45
5.5   React=0.35

# -------------------------------------------------------- 12s: Stacker, Candy
# The falling-block game, and the one whose differences are the point: ten mixed
# polyominoes, and a clear rule that takes any contiguous run rather than a full
# row -- which is also the only rule that works on a playfield thirty cells wide.
# Speed high so a stack actually accumulates inside six seconds.
12   Game=5
12   Palette=3
12   Grid=0.30
12   Speed=0.88
12   Skill=0.45
12   Difficulty=0.55
12   Round=0.30
12   Gap=0.16
12   Glow=0.50

# ----------------------------------------------------------- 18.5s: Trails, Ice
# Four riders that never stop and never reverse. Deaths are resolved
# simultaneously, which is why a head-on kills both and not just the second one
# the loop happened to reach.
18.5  Game=9
18.5  Palette=2
18.5  Grid=0.415
18.5  Speed=0.80
18.5  Skill=0.80
18.5  Difficulty=1.0
18.5  Round=0.22
18.5  Gap=0.14
18.5  Glow=0.55

# -------------------------------------------------------- 25s: Girders, Fire
# Flat floors, alternating roll direction, hazards that cascade back down them.
# The slope in the original is what made barrels roll; at this resolution a slope
# is a staircase, so the direction alternates instead.
25  Game=7
25  Palette=5
25  Grid=0.35
25  Speed=0.70
25  Skill=0.85
25  Difficulty=0.75
25  Round=0.25
25  Gap=0.18
25  Glow=0.50

# ------------------------------------------------------ 30.5s: Swarm, Phosphor
# The formation does not descend. Attackers peel out of it one at a time, fly a
# curved run at the ship, and come back round to their own slot.
30.5  Game=8
30.5  Palette=0
30.5  Grid=0.33
30.5  Speed=0.55
30.5  Skill=0.80
30.5  Difficulty=0.65
30.5  Round=0.28
30.5  Gap=0.16
30.5  Glow=0.50
30.5  React=0.45

# --------------------------------------------------------- 36s: Reflex, Ice
# A hazard closes, a prompt opens, and the bar under the arrow is the remaining
# distance drawn out rather than a second clock counting the same thing.
36  Game=10
36  Palette=2
36  Grid=0.33
36  Speed=0.55
36  Skill=0.90
36  Difficulty=0.35
36  Round=0.25
36  Gap=0.18
36  Glow=0.45

# ------------------------------------------------------- 41s: Rafters, Mono
# Hit the floor from underneath and whatever is standing on it goes over. Mono
# because the mechanic is a silhouette -- upright or on its back -- and no hue
# is doing any of that work.
41  Game=11
41  Palette=4
41  Grid=0.35
41  Speed=0.60
41  Skill=0.90
41  Difficulty=0.60
41  Round=0.25
41  Gap=0.16
41  Glow=0.42

# ---------------------------------------------------------- 45.5s: Duel, Fire
# Wind-up, active, recovery. Only the active window is drawn, because drawing
# the wind-up would tell the other fighter exactly when to block.
45.5  Game=12
45.5  Palette=5
45.5  Grid=0.28
45.5  Speed=0.55
45.5  Skill=0.75
45.5  Difficulty=0.70
45.5  Round=0.30
45.5  Gap=0.18
45.5  Glow=0.55
45.5  React=0.45

# ------------------------------------------------ 50s: the pixel-map ending
# Drop the grid to nineteen cells across and open the gaps. This is what the
# plugin looks like when the composition is a fixture layout and Resolume's
# pixel mapper is sampling it.
#
# On Chase, and that is not an aesthetic choice. Changing `Game` restarts the
# simulation, and the ending needs a full field of cells on its first frame --
# Trails was tried here and a five-second-old light-cycle board is nearly black,
# which is the one thing this shot cannot be. Chase is dense from tick one
# because the maze starts full of pellets.
50  Game=6
50  Palette=5
50  Grid=0.415
50  Speed=0.55
50  Skill=0.90
50  Difficulty=0.35
50  Round=0.20
50  Gap=0.14
50  Glow=0.55

51.5..56  Grid=0.415..0.25
51.5..56  Gap=0.14..0.42
51.5..56  Round=0.20..1.00
51.5..56  Glow=0.55..0.80
